The Last Train Ticket

Every evening at 7:10, the old station clock coughed once and stopped for a breath, as if remembering something it had lost. Aarav stood beneath it every day, pretending to check his phone, pretending he wasn’t waiting.

The ticket counter was closing when he finally stepped forward. Only one ticket remained in the metal tray—destination unreadable, ink faded by too many fingers and too much hope.

“Last one,” the clerk said. “Train leaves in five minutes.”

Aarav hesitated. He hadn’t planned to go anywhere. But staying felt heavier than leaving.

He boarded the train. The coach was nearly empty, except for a woman by the window, humming a tune he hadn’t heard since childhood. She looked up and smiled, like she had been expecting him.

“Running from something?” she asked.

“Maybe,” Aarav replied. “Or toward it.”

The train moved. Outside, the city lights blurred into memories—things unsaid, chances missed, people he never thanked. He felt lighter with every mile.

When the train slowed, the woman stood. “This is your stop,” she said softly.

“But this isn’t my destination,” Aarav said.

She smiled again. “Sometimes, the stop is the destination.”

The doors opened. Morning light flooded in. The station sign read the name of his hometown—small, forgotten, waiting.

When Aarav turned to thank her, the seat was empty.

The train left. Aarav stepped onto the platform, breathing deeply, holding the last train ticket like a promise he was finally ready to keep.
